            "text": "{\"primary_meaning\":\"Eternal conscious torment and ultimate separation from God; the final state of judgment and punishment for the unrepentant and fallen angels.\",\"primary_basis\":[\"Matthew 25:41-46\",\"Mark 9:43-48\",\"Revelation 20:10-15\",\"2 Thessalonians 1:9\",\"Luke 16:19-31\"],\"alternatives\":[{\"meaning\":\"Annihilationism or Conditional Immortality, where the wicked are completely destroyed and cease to exist rather than suffering eternally.\",\"basis\":[\"Matthew 10:28\",\"Romans 6:23\",\"Revelation 20:14\"],\"prominence\":\"minority\"},{\"meaning\":\"Christian Universalism, where Hell is viewed as a temporary, restorative, or purgatorial state that eventually leads to the salvation and reconciliation of all souls to God.\",\"basis\":[\"1 Corinthians 15:22\",\"Colossians 1:20\",\"Romans 5:18\"],\"prominence\":\"minority\"},{\"meaning\":\"A psychological or existential metaphor for the earthly state of spiritual alienation, human suffering, and the consequences of sin without God.\",\"basis\":[\"Romans 1:24-28\",\"John 3:18-19\"],\"prominence\":\"minority\"}],\"has_settled_consensus\":true,\"confidence\":\"high\",\"notes\":\"Historically, eternal conscious torment has been the overwhelmingly dominant consensus across major Christian traditions (Catholic, Eastern Orthodox, and Protestant). In recent decades, alternative views such as conditional immortality and universalism have grown into notable minority positions in academic and theological circles.\"}",
